\import{base-macros}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{The KV cache scales with K, and is per-sequence}
\taxon{Card}
\tag{card}
\meta{anki}{1787063264798}
\meta{anki-deck}{MLSys}

\prompt{How large is a [[TJLA]], and which dimension actually drives it?}{
  The cache has shape #{[2, S, L, K, H]} per sequence — the leading 2 is keys and values — so in int8 it is #{2SLKH} bytes.

  \strong{Worked:} #{S = 8\mathrm{k}}, #{L = 64}, #{KH = D = 8192} gives #{2 \cdot 8192 \cdot 64 \cdot 8192 \approx 8} GiB for a \em{single} sequence.

  \strong{Two things in that expression decide inference planning.} It scales with #{K}, the number of key/value heads, and not with the #{N} query heads — which is the whole argument for [[XUVV]] and [MQA](W4YP) at #{K \ll N}. And it is per sequence, so unlike the weights it cannot be amortised over the batch: it competes with batch size for the same [[AI2G]] budget, and generation is bandwidth-bound on streaming it.
}
